+\emph{3) Local Feature Extraction and Reassembly:}
+In parallel with weight prediction, Unfold extracts a $k\times k$ neighborhood around each input location. Nearest-neighbor interpolation then replicates these neighborhoods onto the higher-resolution grid to align them with the predicted reassembly weights. For $s=2$, the four output locations corresponding to an input location share the same neighborhood but use their own predicted weights. Each output location aggregates the aligned neighborhood as
+\begin{equation}
+Y_{c,i,j}=\sum_{u=1}^{k^2}K_{i,j,u}P_{c,u,i,j}.
+\label{eq:cafr-reassembly}
+\end{equation}
+Here, $P\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times k^2\times sH\times sW}$ denotes the neighborhoods after replication and alignment, $u$ indexes the $k^2$ positions within each neighborhood, and $c$ denotes the feature channel. The predicted weights are shared across feature channels, producing $Y\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times sH\times sW}$. Nearest-neighbor interpolation is therefore used only for neighborhood alignment; the final output is obtained through content-adaptive weighted aggregation, allowing the four output locations to produce different feature responses.
